ficance of the turnpike; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the distance that the turnpike covered. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that the turnpike connected two Pennsylvania cities, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the specific distance covered by the turnpike. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes when the turnpike was built; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the distance that the turnpike covered. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-732-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"c2221658-d2c5-4b78-901e-e8e6ce9c211a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$273c2f12$SATQ$, $SATQ$93751b2c-f263-4e49-91bb-3afc830dc6fd$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Jeffrey Gibson&rsquo;s sculptural object <em>KNOW YOUR MAGIC, BABY,</em> an Everlast-brand exercise bag embroidered with multicolored beads and a fringe associated with the dances of the Ojibwe people, stitches together&mdash;literally and figuratively&mdash;recognizable symbols from both Native and non-Native cultures. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Gibson&rsquo;s piece also blurs the distinction between contemporary art and traditional crafts.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4b726ab1-af11-49e7-a260-420e06281e6c","content":"<p>Conversely,</p>"},{"id":"86b883c7-3453-4a59-a3cf-adf0cad6692a","content":"<p>In so doing,</p>"},{"id":"c0eb1a18-5cb7-4b69-bb70-acc807dedd99","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"697d019d-0f21-45a6-8235-987194076ccc","content":"<p>In particular,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["86b883c7-3453-4a59-a3cf-adf0cad6692a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "In so doing" logically signals that the information in this sentence about Gibson&rsquo;s piece&mdash;that it blurs the distinction between contemporary art and traditional crafts&mdash;is a result or consequence of the piece&rsquo;s blending of particular Native and non-Native cultural symbols.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. "Conversely" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the previous information about the blending of particular cultural symbols in Gibson&rsquo;s piece. Instead, it presents a result or consequence of that information. Choice C is incorrect. "For instance" illogically signals that this sentence provides an example supporting the previous information about the blending of particular cultural symbols in Gibson&rsquo;s piece. Instead, it presents a result or consequence of that information. Choice D is incorrect. "In particular" illogically signals that this sentence provides specific details elaborating on the previous information about the blending of particular cultural symbols in Gibson&rsquo;s piece. Instead, it presents a result or consequence of that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"273c2f12","external_id":"93751b2c-f263-4e49-91bb-3afc830dc6fd","vaultid":null,"uId":"b6382887-4902-4349-b651-2999a28a53a3","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Jeffrey Gibson&rsquo;s sculptural object <em>KNOW YOUR MAGIC, BABY,</em> an Everlast-brand exercise bag embroidered with multicolored beads and a fringe associated with the dances of the Ojibwe people, stitches together&mdash;literally and figuratively&mdash;recognizable symbols from both Native and non-Native cultures. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Gibson&rsquo;s piece also blurs the distinction between contemporary art and traditional crafts.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4b726ab1-af11-49e7-a260-420e06281e6c","content":"<p>Conversely,</p>"},{"id":"86b883c7-3453-4a59-a3cf-adf0cad6692a","content":"<p>In so doing,</p>"},{"id":"c0eb1a18-5cb7-4b69-bb70-acc807dedd99","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"697d019d-0f21-45a6-8235-987194076ccc","content":"<p>In particular,</p>"}],"keys":["86b883c7-3453-4a59-a3cf-adf0cad6692a"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"In so doing\" logically signals that the information in this sentence about Gibson&rsquo;s piece&mdash;that it blurs the distinction between contemporary art and traditional crafts&mdash;is a result or consequence of the piece&rsquo;s blending of particular Native and non-Native cultural symbols.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. \"Conversely\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the previous information about the blending of particular cultural symbols in Gibson&rsquo;s piece. Instead, it presents a result or consequence of that information. Choice C is incorrect. \"For instance\" illogically signals that this sentence provides an example supporting the previous information about the blending of particular cultural symbols in Gibson&rsquo;s piece. Instead, it presents a result or consequence of that information. Choice D is incorrect. \"In particular\" illogically signals that this sentence provides specific details elaborating on the previous information about the blending of particular cultural symbols in Gibson&rsquo;s piece. Instead, it presents a result or consequence of that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$28a46cb0$SATQ$, $SATQ$805f17cc-a0bf-4a25-b5f0-4d37189716f2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The international Slow Food movement was founded in 1989 with the signing of the &ldquo;Slow Food Manifesto.&rdquo;</li>
<li>The movement promotes universal access to healthy, high-quality food.</li>
<li>It calls for sustainable food production practices that protect local environments, ecosystems, and biodiversity.</li>
<li>It advocates for fair treatment of and compensation for food production workers.</li>
<li>The Slow Food USA organization was founded in 2000.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to introduce the Slow Food movement to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3f8318f3-3005-4a57-bfeb-c2ba9f4919d3","content":"<p>The international Slow Food movement, founded in 1989, promotes universal access to healthy, high-quality food that is produced sustainably by workers who are treated and compensated fairly.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ea5b04ac-5263-4a74-9c75-f2111b55eb62","content":"<p>The signing of the &ldquo;Slow Food Manifesto&rdquo; in 1989 marked the founding of the international Slow Food movement, while the Slow Food USA organization was founded in 2000.</p>"},{"id":"161d24a5-fd77-447b-a01d-4187f15e4709","content":"<p>The Slow Food movement advocates for food production workers.</p>"},{"id":"3c3cf2bf-7b65-4912-a430-c0cce2be2acc","content":"<p>Goals of the movement include universal access to healthy, high-quality food and sustainable food practices.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3f8318f3-3005-4a57-bfeb-c2ba9f4919d3"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence effectively introduces the Slow Food movement to a new audience, explaining that the movement, which was founded in 1989, promotes universal access to high-quality and healthy food that is produced sustainably by workers who are treated fairly.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence indicates when the international Slow Food movement and the Slow Food USA organization were founded, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the movement to a new audience. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence notes that the Slow Food movement includes advocacy for food production workers, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the movement to a new audience. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence describes some of the goals of the Slow Food movement, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the movement to a new audience.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"28a46cb0","external_id":"805f17cc-a0bf-4a25-b5f0-4d37189716f2","vaultid":null,"uId":"bf763593-a011-49e9-be41-2e1eb452a603","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The international Slow Food movement was founded in 1989 with the signing of the &ldquo;Slow Food Manifesto.&rdquo;</li>\n<li>The movement promotes universal access to healthy, high-quality food.</li>\n<li>It calls for sustainable food production practices that protect local environments, ecosystems, and biodiversity.</li>\n<li>It advocates for fair treatment of and compensation for food production workers.</li>\n<li>The Slow Food USA organization was founded in 2000.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to introduce the Slow Food movement to a new audience. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3f8318f3-3005-4a57-bfeb-c2ba9f4919d3","content":"<p>The international Slow Food movement, founded in 1989, promotes universal access to healthy, high-quality food that is produced sustainably by workers who are treated and compensated fairly.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ea5b04ac-5263-4a74-9c75-f2111b55eb62","content":"<p>The signing of the &ldquo;Slow Food Manifesto&rdquo; in 1989 marked the founding of the international Slow Food movement, while the Slow Food USA organization was founded in 2000.</p>"},{"id":"161d24a5-fd77-447b-a01d-4187f15e4709","content":"<p>The Slow Food movement advocates for food production workers.</p>"},{"id":"3c3cf2bf-7b65-4912-a430-c0cce2be2acc","content":"<p>Goals of the movement include universal access to healthy, high-quality food and sustainable food practices.</p>"}],"keys":["3f8318f3-3005-4a57-bfeb-c2ba9f4919d3"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence effectively introduces the Slow Food movement to a new audience, explaining that the movement, which was founded in 1989, promotes universal access to high-quality and healthy food that is produced sustainably by workers who are treated fairly.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence indicates when the international Slow Food movement and the Slow Food USA organization were founded, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the movement to a new audience. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence notes that the Slow Food movement includes advocacy for food production workers, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the movement to a new audience. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence describes some of the goals of the Slow Food movement, it doesn&rsquo;t effectively introduce the movement to a new audience.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$290a5d77$SATQ$, $SATQ$ccc1496e-1b9b-4ef5-838f-fc626d8a2653$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Guard cells are specialized cells that are part of a plant&rsquo;s pores. These cells help regulate the amount of carbon dioxide a plant takes in. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> they help regulate a plant&rsquo;s water loss.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6135449b-a051-42ac-8f41-53a59f801c00","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"fa6733db-19a9-489d-ac85-e9b7348dd85c","content":"<p>Previously,</p>"},{"id":"540667f0-03aa-4dcf-86c4-7537245623ee","content":"<p>In conclusion,</p>"},{"id":"2e58a5c9-dc7c-4e54-b741-15ad1bbf683e","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6135449b-a051-42ac-8f41-53a59f801c00"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. "Additionally" logically signals that guard cells&rsquo; role in regulating water loss is an additional function of these specialized plant cells that is separate from the function of regulating carbon dioxide intake.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because "previously" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence occurs earlier in a chronological sequence of events than the regulation of carbon dioxide intake described in the previous sentence. Instead, regulating water loss is an additional function of guard cells that is separate from the function of regulating carbon dioxide intake. Choice C is incorrect because "in conclusion" illogically signals that the description of guard cells&rsquo; role in regulating water loss concludes or summarizes the information about guard cells provided in the previous sentences. Instead, regulating water loss is one of the two distinct functions of guard cells described in the text. Choice D is incorrect because "instead" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence happens in place of the activity of regulating carbon dioxide intake described in the previous sentence. Rather, regulating water loss is an additional function of guard cells.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"290a5d77","external_id":"ccc1496e-1b9b-4ef5-838f-fc626d8a2653","vaultid":null,"uId":"820c20ae-07ac-4617-b79e-c5d16bcd4504","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Guard cells are specialized cells that are part of a plant&rsquo;s pores. These cells help regulate the amount of carbon dioxide a plant takes in. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> they help regulate a plant&rsquo;s water loss.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6135449b-a051-42ac-8f41-53a59f801c00","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"fa6733db-19a9-489d-ac85-e9b7348dd85c","content":"<p>Previously,</p>"},{"id":"540667f0-03aa-4dcf-86c4-7537245623ee","content":"<p>In conclusion,</p>"},{"id":"2e58a5c9-dc7c-4e54-b741-15ad1bbf683e","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"}],"keys":["6135449b-a051-42ac-8f41-53a59f801c00"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. \"Additionally\" logically signals that guard cells&rsquo; role in regulating water loss is an additional function of these specialized plant cells that is separate from the function of regulating carbon dioxide intake.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because \"previously\" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence occurs earlier in a chronological sequence of events than the regulation of carbon dioxide intake described in the previous sentence. Instead, regulating water loss is an additional function of guard cells that is separate from the function of regulating carbon dioxide intake. Choice C is incorrect because \"in conclusion\" illogically signals that the description of guard cells&rsquo; role in regulating water loss concludes or summarizes the information about guard cells provided in the previous sentences. Instead, regulating water loss is one of the two distinct functions of guard cells described in the text. Choice D is incorrect because \"instead\" illogically signals that the activity described in this sentence happens in place of the activity of regulating carbon dioxide intake described in the previous sentence. Rather, regulating water loss is an additional function of guard cells.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$296801d2$SATQ$, $SATQ$46cc1e17-c0f3-42bf-93f2-84495322f76a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$07c73eb0-187b-4aaa-9593-620445c0ebf5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Azores is a group of islands about 870 miles off the coast of Portugal.</li>
<li>Historians have long believed that in the fifteenth century Portuguese mariners were the first humans to populate the Azores.</li>
<li>A 2015 study coauthored by Sofia Gabriel and Maria da Luz Mathias found that Vikings from Scandinavia may have populated the Azores as early as the ninth century.</li>
<li>The researchers found a genetic connection between house mice in the Azores and house mice in Scandinavia.</li>
<li>House mice may have traveled from Scandinavia to the Azores on Viking ships.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify who may have first populated the Azores, according to the 2015 study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"75b84669-1c91-4fbc-8820-1be761335cdc","content":"<p>Historians have long believed that the first humans to populate the Azores, a group of islands about 870 miles off the coast of Portugal, arrived in the fifteenth century.</p>"},{"id":"807a2e27-e4a9-4a8c-8d13-8ecf3ff98fdb","content":"<p>Portuguese mariners may not have been the first humans to populate the Azores.</p>"},{"id":"9912f6ee-3808-4b1c-bc55-e7e438de8cc8","content":"<p>In their 2015 study, the researchers found a genetic connection between house mice in the Azores and those in Scandinavia.</p>"},{"id":"7e0e50e4-b634-4c15-9fa3-d77aaaebdb0a","content":"<p>According to a 2015 study, the first humans to populate the Azores may have been Vikings from Scandinavia, not mariners from Portugal as previously believed.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7e0e50e4-b634-4c15-9fa3-d77aaaebdb0a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. This choice effectively specifies who may have first populated the Azores, according to the 2015 study: the Vikings.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t effectively specify who may have first populated the Azores, according to the 2015 study. It only mentions the historical belief that the Portuguese were first. The 2015 study drew a different conclusion. Choice B is incorrect. This choice casts doubt on the Portuguese claim but doesn&rsquo;t name the group of people who may have arrived before the Portuguese. Choice C is incorrect. This choice mentions the evidence that the researchers found but not the conclusion they drew from it. It doesn&rsquo;t name the possible group of people who may have arrived before the Portuguese. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-SYN-E008$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ea2ac1be-940f-4364-bd67-aecceb56ccd3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"296801d2","external_id":"46cc1e17-c0f3-42bf-93f2-84495322f76a","vaultid":"07c73eb0-187b-4aaa-9593-620445c0ebf5","uId":"206b8e7d-793a-4c6b-83e8-c122e33527e7","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Azores is a group of islands about 870 miles off the coast of Portugal.</li>\n<li>Historians have long believed that in the fifteenth century Portuguese mariners were the first humans to populate the Azores.</li>\n<li>A 2015 study coauthored by Sofia Gabriel and Maria da Luz Mathias found that Vikings from Scandinavia may have populated the Azores as early as the ninth century.</li>\n<li>The researchers found a genetic connection between house mice in the Azores and house mice in Scandinavia.</li>\n<li>House mice may have traveled from Scandinavia to the Azores on Viking ships.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify who may have first populated the Azores, according to the 2015 study. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"75b84669-1c91-4fbc-8820-1be761335cdc","content":"<p>Historians have long believed that the first humans to populate the Azores, a group of islands about 870 miles off the coast of Portugal, arrived in the fifteenth century.</p>"},{"id":"807a2e27-e4a9-4a8c-8d13-8ecf3ff98fdb","content":"<p>Portuguese mariners may not have been the first humans to populate the Azores.</p>"},{"id":"9912f6ee-3808-4b1c-bc55-e7e438de8cc8","content":"<p>In their 2015 study, the researchers found a genetic connection between house mice in the Azores and those in Scandinavia.</p>"},{"id":"7e0e50e4-b634-4c15-9fa3-d77aaaebdb0a","content":"<p>According to a 2015 study, the first humans to populate the Azores may have been Vikings from Scandinavia, not mariners from Portugal as previously believed.</p>"}],"keys":["7e0e50e4-b634-4c15-9fa3-d77aaaebdb0a"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. This choice effectively specifies who may have first populated the Azores, according to the 2015 study: the Vikings.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice doesn&rsquo;t effectively specify who may have first populated the Azores, according to the 2015 study. It only mentions the historical belief that the Portuguese were first. The 2015 study drew a different conclusion. Choice B is incorrect. This choice casts doubt on the Portuguese claim but doesn&rsquo;t name the group of people who may have arrived before the Portuguese. Choice C is incorrect. This choice mentions the evidence that the researchers found but not the conclusion they drew from it. It doesn&rsquo;t name the possible group of people who may have arrived before the Portuguese. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-SYN-E008","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ea2ac1be-940f-4364-bd67-aecceb56ccd3","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959371,"updateDate":1691007959371}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959371, 1691007959371),
    ($SATQ$29ae4d48$SATQ$, $SATQ$5ed926eb-3044-40cb-8edd-c39c901a8b19$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the early 1970s, Albert Popa took up graffiti art, spraying his work onto what was at the time an unconventional surface: concrete. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Albert&rsquo;s son David has chosen an unusual canvas for his new art project, <em>Fractured</em>. In this remarkable work, the artist draws charcoal faces onto fragmented ice floes in Finland, creating the visual effect of a face slowly fracturing.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2f8ae770-e9c5-4844-bad1-9f140db24e5f","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"26b47257-be18-4807-b87b-6ce1e3ebb1fb","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"0b243d35-99a7-4efb-b552-2a25bee88f67","content":"<p>Second,</p>"},{"id":"a076da0f-4d16-4925-8d17-552ed8e47261","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a076da0f-4d16-4925-8d17-552ed8e47261"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Likewise" logically signals that the information about Albert&rsquo;s son David is similar to the previous information about Albert Popa. Both artists have used unconventional surfaces for their work: Albert used concrete, and David is using ice floes.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that the information about David contrasts with the previous information about Albert Popa. Instead, it is similar to the previous information about Albert Popa. Choice B is incorrect because "indeed" illogically signals that the information about David emphasizes or strengthens the previous point about Albert Popa. Instead, it is similar to the previous information; it highlights a similarity between father and son. Choice C is incorrect because "second" illogically signals that the information about David is a second point or reason separate from the previous information about Albert Popa. Instead, it is similar to the previous information; it highlights a similarity between father and son.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"29ae4d48","external_id":"5ed926eb-3044-40cb-8edd-c39c901a8b19","vaultid":null,"uId":"4d76e6b1-924d-4765-87bf-aa382aede493","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the early 1970s, Albert Popa took up graffiti art, spraying his work onto what was at the time an unconventional surface: concrete. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Albert&rsquo;s son David has chosen an unusual canvas for his new art project, <em>Fractured</em>. In this remarkable work, the artist draws charcoal faces onto fragmented ice floes in Finland, creating the visual effect of a face slowly fracturing.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2f8ae770-e9c5-4844-bad1-9f140db24e5f","content":"<p>However,</p>"},{"id":"26b47257-be18-4807-b87b-6ce1e3ebb1fb","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"0b243d35-99a7-4efb-b552-2a25bee88f67","content":"<p>Second,</p>"},{"id":"a076da0f-4d16-4925-8d17-552ed8e47261","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"}],"keys":["a076da0f-4d16-4925-8d17-552ed8e47261"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Likewise\" logically signals that the information about Albert&rsquo;s son David is similar to the previous information about Albert Popa. Both artists have used unconventional surfaces for their work: Albert used concrete, and David is using ice floes.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that the information about David contrasts with the previous information about Albert Popa. Instead, it is similar to the previous information about Albert Popa. Choice B is incorrect because \"indeed\" illogically signals that the information about David emphasizes or strengthens the previous point about Albert Popa. Instead, it is similar to the previous information; it highlights a similarity between father and son. Choice C is incorrect because \"second\" illogically signals that the information about David is a second point or reason separate from the previous information about Albert Popa. Instead, it is similar to the previous information; it highlights a similarity between father and son.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$2b08f514$SATQ$, $SATQ$5f5a1b2c-faa5-422e-a8fa-1db3b693a78e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The prime meridian, the global indicator of zero degrees longitude established in 1884, was originally determined using astronomically derived coordinates. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> as decades passed, new calculations would reveal increasingly precise coordinates, yet the prime meridian remained unchanged; it wasn&rsquo;t until the 1980s that, spurred by improved geodetic data, the prime meridian was officially moved&mdash;roughly one hundred meters east.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4a84b53f-b2bf-4ca7-9e67-035a7c4d555b","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"e006217a-e157-4937-b7fd-064c9ac19fc4","content":"<p>To that end,&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"16d5d6d9-0224-4b35-9a02-94055da70a93","content":"<p>Again and again,</p>"},{"id":"8a55de9f-a59e-48db-8d41-67f7be193be6","content":"<p>Granted,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["16d5d6d9-0224-4b35-9a02-94055da70a93"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "Again and again" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that new calculations revealed increasingly precise coordinates for the location of the prime meridian&mdash;refers to events that occurred multiple times in the decades after the establishment of the prime meridian in 1884 (which is described in the preceding sentence).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "specifically" illogically signals that the information in this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on the description of the prime meridian&rsquo;s establishment in the previous sentence. Instead, the sentence indicates that increasingly precise coordinates were revealed on multiple occasions in the decades following the meridian&rsquo;s establishment. Choice B is incorrect because "to that end" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a means of accomplishing a goal established in the previous sentence about the prime meridian&rsquo;s establishment. Instead, the sentence indicates that increasingly precise coordinates were revealed on multiple occasions in the decades following the meridian&rsquo;s establishment. Choice D is incorrect because "granted" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is in opposition to the information about the prime meridian&rsquo;s establishment in the previous sentence. Instead, the sentence indicates that increasingly precise coordinates were revealed on multiple occasions in the decades following the meridian&rsquo;s establishment.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2b08f514","external_id":"5f5a1b2c-faa5-422e-a8fa-1db3b693a78e","vaultid":null,"uId":"008db065-5d48-4f4b-9195-d338ae2a9281","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The prime meridian, the global indicator of zero degrees longitude established in 1884, was originally determined using astronomically derived coordinates. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> as decades passed, new calculations would reveal increasingly precise coordinates, yet the prime meridian remained unchanged; it wasn&rsquo;t until the 1980s that, spurred by improved geodetic data, the prime meridian was officially moved&mdash;roughly one hundred meters east.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4a84b53f-b2bf-4ca7-9e67-035a7c4d555b","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"e006217a-e157-4937-b7fd-064c9ac19fc4","content":"<p>To that end,&nbsp; &nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"16d5d6d9-0224-4b35-9a02-94055da70a93","content":"<p>Again and again,</p>"},{"id":"8a55de9f-a59e-48db-8d41-67f7be193be6","content":"<p>Granted,</p>"}],"keys":["16d5d6d9-0224-4b35-9a02-94055da70a93"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"Again and again\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that new calculations revealed increasingly precise coordinates for the location of the prime meridian&mdash;refers to events that occurred multiple times in the decades after the establishment of the prime meridian in 1884 (which is described in the preceding sentence).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"specifically\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence provides specific, precise details elaborating on the description of the prime meridian&rsquo;s establishment in the previous sentence. Instead, the sentence indicates that increasingly precise coordinates were revealed on multiple occasions in the decades following the meridian&rsquo;s establishment. Choice B is incorrect because \"to that end\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a means of accomplishing a goal established in the previous sentence about the prime meridian&rsquo;s establishment. Instead, the sentence indicates that increasingly precise coordinates were revealed on multiple occasions in the decades following the meridian&rsquo;s establishment. Choice D is incorrect because \"granted\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is in opposition to the information about the prime meridian&rsquo;s establishment in the previous sentence. Instead, the sentence indicates that increasingly precise coordinates were revealed on multiple occasions in the decades following the meridian&rsquo;s establishment.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$2b5e0731$SATQ$, $SATQ$b0615a31-d511-4df2-9114-0d060c03de94$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ef206a1d-6878-4a87-8474-31cb213861fc$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>With darkness falling, a mother elephant loses sight of her calf and wants to make sure it is safe. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> she releases an infrasonic call for the calf to hear. Infrasonic sound is below the range of human hearing, but many animals can hear these sounds from several miles away.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2512309f-d918-48ac-82fc-02405a8dcf43","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"49c93408-c738-47e4-a44c-ff0cf5353c91","content":"<p>For this reason,</p>"},{"id":"458ea192-4e2f-4f84-a157-0577e83cd78d","content":"<p>Nowadays,</p>"},{"id":"df8940d4-7f7c-4d44-bcd6-f5bf7b34f9ae","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["49c93408-c738-47e4-a44c-ff0cf5353c91"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "For this reason" is a cause-and-effect transition. The cause in this case is that the mother elephant wants to know that her calf is safe, so the effect is that she lets out an infrasonic call for the calf to hear. Therefore, "for this reason" fits perfectly in this context.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that introduces an example of a previous idea. But the second sentence doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of the events described in the first sentence. Instead, it describes what happens next: the mother elephant calls for her calf. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates a shift from the past to the current time, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. Both sentences use the present tense, as they&rsquo;re describing the same time period. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates commonality or agreement between two ideas. But this sentence isn&rsquo;t similar to the events in the first sentence. Instead, it describes the events that happen next.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-E009$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$24eaef90-f5a5-4aa4-8ae5-f4e869a1d2b5$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2b5e0731","external_id":"b0615a31-d511-4df2-9114-0d060c03de94","vaultid":"ef206a1d-6878-4a87-8474-31cb213861fc","uId":"baa99dab-a122-415d-8052-76871b65a883","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>With darkness falling, a mother elephant loses sight of her calf and wants to make sure it is safe. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> she releases an infrasonic call for the calf to hear. Infrasonic sound is below the range of human hearing, but many animals can hear these sounds from several miles away.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2512309f-d918-48ac-82fc-02405a8dcf43","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"49c93408-c738-47e4-a44c-ff0cf5353c91","content":"<p>For this reason,</p>"},{"id":"458ea192-4e2f-4f84-a157-0577e83cd78d","content":"<p>Nowadays,</p>"},{"id":"df8940d4-7f7c-4d44-bcd6-f5bf7b34f9ae","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}],"keys":["49c93408-c738-47e4-a44c-ff0cf5353c91"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"For this reason\" is a cause-and-effect transition. The cause in this case is that the mother elephant wants to know that her calf is safe, so the effect is that she lets out an infrasonic call for the calf to hear. Therefore, \"for this reason\" fits perfectly in this context.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that introduces an example of a previous idea. But the second sentence doesn&rsquo;t provide an example of the events described in the first sentence. Instead, it describes what happens next: the mother elephant calls for her calf. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates a shift from the past to the current time, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. Both sentences use the present tense, as they&rsquo;re describing the same time period. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates commonality or agreement between two ideas. But this sentence isn&rsquo;t similar to the events in the first sentence. Instead, it describes the events that happen next.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-E009","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"24eaef90-f5a5-4aa4-8ae5-f4e869a1d2b5","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$2b5f4bdc$SATQ$, $SATQ$5747e563-791b-4613-91ea-2212b380a4b6$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$cd4a3986-b252-431f-b609-3d94c7274816$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In the early 1900s, Jovita Id&aacute;r fought injustice on both sides of the Mexico&ndash;United States border. As a reporter for the Texas newspaper <em>La Cr&oacute;nica,</em> she voiced support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt against authoritarian rule. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> she founded the League of Mexican Women, a group that advocated for the rights of Mexican Americans.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bb9860e9-bcbd-4c28-8c20-c3a62b4502cc","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"09aa90ac-744e-413c-8a43-3fd706eb2fd6","content":"<p>In conclusion,</p>"},{"id":"54bd47a6-97cc-478b-8c50-61d5327e8e47","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"b30a2471-eb58-430a-a08b-05655717dd69","content":"<p>Rather,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["bb9860e9-bcbd-4c28-8c20-c3a62b4502cc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because &ldquo;additionally&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that Id&aacute;r founded the League of Mexican Women&mdash;is another instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice, this time advocating for the rights of Mexican Americans. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;in conclusion&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence sums up or concludes the discussion of Id&aacute;r&rsquo;s support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt. Instead, the founding of the League of Mexican Women is a separate instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an example of how, as a newspaper reporter, Id&aacute;r voiced support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt. Instead, the founding of the League of Mexican Women is a separate instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice, this time in support of Mexican Americans.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;rather&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence offers a contrast or exception to the previous information about Id&aacute;r&rsquo;s support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt. Instead, the founding of the League of Mexican Women is a separate instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-267-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$47f7216d-6a34-4a91-b876-fe3be11d27ec$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2b5f4bdc","external_id":"5747e563-791b-4613-91ea-2212b380a4b6","vaultid":"cd4a3986-b252-431f-b609-3d94c7274816","uId":"ca94cd49-166c-4eac-8ba3-548d4a8fe760","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In the early 1900s, Jovita Id&aacute;r fought injustice on both sides of the Mexico&ndash;United States border. As a reporter for the Texas newspaper <em>La Cr&oacute;nica,</em> she voiced support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt against authoritarian rule. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> she founded the League of Mexican Women, a group that advocated for the rights of Mexican Americans.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bb9860e9-bcbd-4c28-8c20-c3a62b4502cc","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"09aa90ac-744e-413c-8a43-3fd706eb2fd6","content":"<p>In conclusion,</p>"},{"id":"54bd47a6-97cc-478b-8c50-61d5327e8e47","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"b30a2471-eb58-430a-a08b-05655717dd69","content":"<p>Rather,</p>"}],"keys":["bb9860e9-bcbd-4c28-8c20-c3a62b4502cc"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because &ldquo;additionally&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that Id&aacute;r founded the League of Mexican Women&mdash;is another instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice, this time advocating for the rights of Mexican Americans. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;in conclusion&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence sums up or concludes the discussion of Id&aacute;r&rsquo;s support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt. Instead, the founding of the League of Mexican Women is a separate instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an example of how, as a newspaper reporter, Id&aacute;r voiced support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt. Instead, the founding of the League of Mexican Women is a separate instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice, this time in support of Mexican Americans.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;rather&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence offers a contrast or exception to the previous information about Id&aacute;r&rsquo;s support for the Mexican people&rsquo;s revolt. Instead, the founding of the League of Mexican Women is a separate instance of Id&aacute;r fighting injustice. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-267-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"47f7216d-6a34-4a91-b876-fe3be11d27ec","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959334,"updateDate":1691007959334}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959334, 1691007959334),
    ($SATQ$2b89bfe5$SATQ$, $SATQ$f16cc16a-a228-411c-b25e-e1d4a04e2cdb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$c9782562-fc18-4214-b4e3-3619baeedd44$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In 1999, astronomer Todd Henry studied the differences in surface temperature between the Sun and nearby stars.</li>
<li>His team mapped all stars within 10 parsecs (approximately 200 trillion miles) of the Sun.</li>
<li>The surface temperature of the Sun is around 9,800&deg;F, which classifies it as a G star.</li>
<li>327 of the 357 stars in the study were classified as K or M stars, with surface temperatures under 8,900&deg;F (cooler than the Sun).</li>
<li>11 of the 357 stars in the study were classified as A or F stars, with surface temperatures greater than 10,300&deg;F (hotter than the Sun).</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"477bfa02-d215-4c7b-86e6-7bd76b20dbcc","content":"<p>At around 9,800&deg;F, which classifies it as a G star, the Sun is hotter than most but not all of the stars within 10 parsecs of it.</p>"},{"id":"47aa4891-7a7e-4920-a3e1-c97f087298d3","content":"<p>Astronomer Todd Henry determined that the Sun, at around 9,800&deg;F, is a G star, and several other stars within a 10-parsec range are A or F stars.</p>"},{"id":"d3de300a-304e-44a4-881c-e43bbf95b5d1","content":"<p>Of the 357 stars within ten parsecs of the Sun, 327 are classified as K or M stars, with surface temperatures under 8,900&deg;F.</p>"},{"id":"f1bb066e-3298-41c9-b987-1ca71c14d125","content":"<p>While most of the stars within 10 parsecs of the Sun are classified as K, M, A, or F stars, the Sun is classified as a G star due to its surface temperature of 9,800&deg;F.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["477bfa02-d215-4c7b-86e6-7bd76b20dbcc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. Noting that the Sun (9,800&deg;F) is hotter than most stars within 10 parsecs of it, the sentence emphasizes how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence explains that astronomer Todd Henry determined the classifications for the Sun and several other stars nearby; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains that the majority of stars near the Sun are classified as K or M stars; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate the Sun&rsquo;s temperature or emphasize how hot it is relative to nearby stars. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence indicates that the Sun is classified differently than most nearby stars due to its surface temperature, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-751-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$78162dc0-f213-4759-aac4-72e351b32a20$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2b89bfe5","external_id":"f16cc16a-a228-411c-b25e-e1d4a04e2cdb","vaultid":"c9782562-fc18-4214-b4e3-3619baeedd44","uId":"6f1f33ae-00a4-452b-999c-3dc1ae49458c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In 1999, astronomer Todd Henry studied the differences in surface temperature between the Sun and nearby stars.</li>\n<li>His team mapped all stars within 10 parsecs (approximately 200 trillion miles) of the Sun.</li>\n<li>The surface temperature of the Sun is around 9,800&deg;F, which classifies it as a G star.</li>\n<li>327 of the 357 stars in the study were classified as K or M stars, with surface temperatures under 8,900&deg;F (cooler than the Sun).</li>\n<li>11 of the 357 stars in the study were classified as A or F stars, with surface temperatures greater than 10,300&deg;F (hotter than the Sun).</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"477bfa02-d215-4c7b-86e6-7bd76b20dbcc","content":"<p>At around 9,800&deg;F, which classifies it as a G star, the Sun is hotter than most but not all of the stars within 10 parsecs of it.</p>"},{"id":"47aa4891-7a7e-4920-a3e1-c97f087298d3","content":"<p>Astronomer Todd Henry determined that the Sun, at around 9,800&deg;F, is a G star, and several other stars within a 10-parsec range are A or F stars.</p>"},{"id":"d3de300a-304e-44a4-881c-e43bbf95b5d1","content":"<p>Of the 357 stars within ten parsecs of the Sun, 327 are classified as K or M stars, with surface temperatures under 8,900&deg;F.</p>"},{"id":"f1bb066e-3298-41c9-b987-1ca71c14d125","content":"<p>While most of the stars within 10 parsecs of the Sun are classified as K, M, A, or F stars, the Sun is classified as a G star due to its surface temperature of 9,800&deg;F.</p>"}],"keys":["477bfa02-d215-4c7b-86e6-7bd76b20dbcc"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. Noting that the Sun (9,800&deg;F) is hotter than most stars within 10 parsecs of it, the sentence emphasizes how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence explains that astronomer Todd Henry determined the classifications for the Sun and several other stars nearby; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains that the majority of stars near the Sun are classified as K or M stars; it doesn&rsquo;t indicate the Sun&rsquo;s temperature or emphasize how hot it is relative to nearby stars. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence indicates that the Sun is classified differently than most nearby stars due to its surface temperature, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize how hot the Sun is relative to nearby stars. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-751-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"78162dc0-f213-4759-aac4-72e351b32a20","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$2ba97187$SATQ$, $SATQ$ba254f3b-4864-4414-bb3d-2331683558d7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Upon first approaching artist Kurt Wenner&rsquo;s <em>Dies Irae</em>, a colorful scene painted on the surface of a cobblestone street in Mantua, Italy, one might assume a deep hole filled with life-sized, classically styled sculptures had opened up in the street. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> by expertly applying the principles of perspective, Wenner created merely the illusion of depth.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"49b4e70b-012f-49fa-86f8-1a0a0a829f19","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"d131670f-77b4-4230-8565-a8ef5c5cdc2a","content":"<p>On the contrary,</p>"},{"id":"e86aedf9-f2a4-4453-be50-10f170fdcdb9","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"d8d8aacf-541d-48dc-a943-9d0d0c924f90","content":"<p>Next,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d131670f-77b4-4230-8565-a8ef5c5cdc2a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "On the contrary" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that <em>Dies Irae</em>&rsquo;s appearance of depth is merely an illusion&mdash;contrasts with the previous statement about a viewer&rsquo;s possible assumption regarding the street painting.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "additionally" illogically signals that this sentence is simply additional information about a viewer&rsquo;s possible assumption regarding the street painting. Instead, the information about how Wenner achieved the illusion of depth contrasts with the previous sentence&rsquo;s description of the illusion. Choice C is incorrect because "as a result" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result of, or caused by, a viewer&rsquo;s possible assumption regarding the street painting. Instead, the information about how Wenner achieved the illusion of depth contrasts with the previous sentence&rsquo;s description of the illusion. Choice D is incorrect because "next" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is the next step in a process. Instead, the information about how Wenner achieved the illusion of depth contrasts with the previous sentence&rsquo;s description of the illusion.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2ba97187","external_id":"ba254f3b-4864-4414-bb3d-2331683558d7","vaultid":null,"uId":"ecf4ecfd-2161-49cf-86c7-93f632893d94","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Upon first approaching artist Kurt Wenner&rsquo;s <em>Dies Irae</em>, a colorful scene painted on the surface of a cobblestone street in Mantua, Italy, one might assume a deep hole filled with life-sized, classically styled sculptures had opened up in the street. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> by expertly applying the principles of perspective, Wenner created merely the illusion of depth.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"49b4e70b-012f-49fa-86f8-1a0a0a829f19","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"d131670f-77b4-4230-8565-a8ef5c5cdc2a","content":"<p>On the contrary,</p>"},{"id":"e86aedf9-f2a4-4453-be50-10f170fdcdb9","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"d8d8aacf-541d-48dc-a943-9d0d0c924f90","content":"<p>Next,</p>"}],"keys":["d131670f-77b4-4230-8565-a8ef5c5cdc2a"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"On the contrary\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that <em>Dies Irae</em>&rsquo;s appearance of depth is merely an illusion&mdash;contrasts with the previous statement about a viewer&rsquo;s possible assumption regarding the street painting.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"additionally\" illogically signals that this sentence is simply additional information about a viewer&rsquo;s possible assumption regarding the street painting. Instead, the information about how Wenner achieved the illusion of depth contrasts with the previous sentence&rsquo;s description of the illusion. Choice C is incorrect because \"as a result\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a result of, or caused by, a viewer&rsquo;s possible assumption regarding the street painting. Instead, the information about how Wenner achieved the illusion of depth contrasts with the previous sentence&rsquo;s description of the illusion. Choice D is incorrect because \"next\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is the next step in a process. Instead, the information about how Wenner achieved the illusion of depth contrasts with the previous sentence&rsquo;s description of the illusion.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669212,"updateDate":1743430669212}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669212, 1743430669212),
    ($SATQ$2bda9edb$SATQ$, $SATQ$75904fc7-284b-4151-b762-3d901c50d7f8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$62f32914-e014-4d2e-b5fa-e0eff2dd490a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 1885, Chinese-born California resident Mary Tape became a hero of the Asian American civil rights movement. In January of that year, she won an antidiscrimination case in the California Supreme Court. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in April, she wrote an open letter criticizing her local board of education for discrimination. Both actions are remembered today as historic stands against racism.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"277e4424-bcf9-4931-82e0-c9164cff7c5e","content":"<p>Later,</p>"},{"id":"431a2bb1-2d85-4c42-9b1c-f704cd31fbcd","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"43ebdc55-565c-4031-9d83-cc025de5ac53","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"},{"id":"b15b469a-60ba-423a-875a-a8e93cee5a4e","content":"<p>Rather,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["277e4424-bcf9-4931-82e0-c9164cff7c5e"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Later&rdquo; logically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence occurred later in a chronological sequence of events than did the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; illogically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence is an example of the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. Instead, the letter-writing is an event that occurred after the court case. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;in other words&rdquo; illogically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. Instead, the letter-writing is an event that occurred after the court case. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;rather&rdquo; illogically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence is an alternative to the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. Instead, the letter-writing is an event that occurred after the court case. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-260-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$49cdf444-e900-4fba-950d-6bd24009e55a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2bda9edb","external_id":"75904fc7-284b-4151-b762-3d901c50d7f8","vaultid":"62f32914-e014-4d2e-b5fa-e0eff2dd490a","uId":"dc8834ff-6bc5-4e2c-88dd-09f16c94375c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 1885, Chinese-born California resident Mary Tape became a hero of the Asian American civil rights movement. In January of that year, she won an antidiscrimination case in the California Supreme Court. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in April, she wrote an open letter criticizing her local board of education for discrimination. Both actions are remembered today as historic stands against racism.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"277e4424-bcf9-4931-82e0-c9164cff7c5e","content":"<p>Later,</p>"},{"id":"431a2bb1-2d85-4c42-9b1c-f704cd31fbcd","content":"<p>For instance,</p>"},{"id":"43ebdc55-565c-4031-9d83-cc025de5ac53","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"},{"id":"b15b469a-60ba-423a-875a-a8e93cee5a4e","content":"<p>Rather,</p>"}],"keys":["277e4424-bcf9-4931-82e0-c9164cff7c5e"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. &ldquo;Later&rdquo; logically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence occurred later in a chronological sequence of events than did the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;for instance&rdquo; illogically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence is an example of the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. Instead, the letter-writing is an event that occurred after the court case. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;in other words&rdquo; illogically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. Instead, the letter-writing is an event that occurred after the court case. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;rather&rdquo; illogically signals that the letter-writing discussed in this sentence is an alternative to the antidiscrimination case discussed in the previous sentence. Instead, the letter-writing is an event that occurred after the court case. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-260-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"49cdf444-e900-4fba-950d-6bd24009e55a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959334,"updateDate":1691007959334}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959334, 1691007959334),
    ($SATQ$2bf05ae9$SATQ$, $SATQ$e5f3aa2b-0b6a-4620-8f53-9833c209cfc8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$37e9683b-f39b-4298-b967-ec0dba5fd286$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>In the midst of the US Civil War, Susie Taylor escaped slavery and fled to Union-army-occupied St. Simons Island off the Georgia coast.</li>
<li>She began working for an all-Black army regiment as a nurse and teacher.</li>
<li>In 1902, she published a book about the time she spent with the regiment.</li>
<li>Her book was the only Civil War memoir to be published by a Black woman.</li>
<li>It is still available to readers in print and online.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the uniqueness of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"37034507-e34c-4fe1-91ff-01625ebabb99","content":"<p>Taylor fled to St. Simons Island, which was then occupied by the Union army, for whom she began working.</p>"},{"id":"cd72c79b-bb8d-4d62-9e1e-3b65ccb5a441","content":"<p>After escaping slavery, Taylor began working for an all-Black army regiment as a nurse and teacher.</p>"},{"id":"0f4909ac-2934-483a-9333-d43dae84eb82","content":"<p>The book Taylor wrote about the time she spent with the regiment is still available to readers in print and online.</p>"},{"id":"ad9cfa95-fa01-411f-b363-5782a1da9bdc","content":"<p>Taylor was the only Black woman to publish a Civil War memoir.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ad9cfa95-fa01-411f-b363-5782a1da9bdc"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. By indicating that Taylor&rsquo;s book was the only Civil War memoir published by a Black woman, this sentence emphasizes the uniqueness, or one-of-a-kind nature, of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishment. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence describes some of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishments, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the uniqueness of them. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence describes some of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishments, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize that they were unique. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about Taylor&rsquo;s book, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize what made the book unique. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-788-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$e576966e-6d5c-4f94-975d-3a38e615a356$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2bf05ae9","external_id":"e5f3aa2b-0b6a-4620-8f53-9833c209cfc8","vaultid":"37e9683b-f39b-4298-b967-ec0dba5fd286","uId":"2afd02de-45ed-4b21-ba94-070367248159","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the midst of the US Civil War, Susie Taylor escaped slavery and fled to Union-army-occupied St. Simons Island off the Georgia coast.</li>\n<li>She began working for an all-Black army regiment as a nurse and teacher.</li>\n<li>In 1902, she published a book about the time she spent with the regiment.</li>\n<li>Her book was the only Civil War memoir to be published by a Black woman.</li>\n<li>It is still available to readers in print and online.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the uniqueness of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishment. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"37034507-e34c-4fe1-91ff-01625ebabb99","content":"<p>Taylor fled to St. Simons Island, which was then occupied by the Union army, for whom she began working.</p>"},{"id":"cd72c79b-bb8d-4d62-9e1e-3b65ccb5a441","content":"<p>After escaping slavery, Taylor began working for an all-Black army regiment as a nurse and teacher.</p>"},{"id":"0f4909ac-2934-483a-9333-d43dae84eb82","content":"<p>The book Taylor wrote about the time she spent with the regiment is still available to readers in print and online.</p>"},{"id":"ad9cfa95-fa01-411f-b363-5782a1da9bdc","content":"<p>Taylor was the only Black woman to publish a Civil War memoir.</p>"}],"keys":["ad9cfa95-fa01-411f-b363-5782a1da9bdc"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. By indicating that Taylor&rsquo;s book was the only Civil War memoir published by a Black woman, this sentence emphasizes the uniqueness, or one-of-a-kind nature, of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishment. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence describes some of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishments, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the uniqueness of them. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence describes some of Taylor&rsquo;s accomplishments, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize that they were unique. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about Taylor&rsquo;s book, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize what made the book unique. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-788-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"e576966e-6d5c-4f94-975d-3a38e615a356","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959353,"updateDate":1691007959353}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959353, 1691007959353),
    ($SATQ$2c61e0b9$SATQ$, $SATQ$91d983a0-c121-4eb2-a3cf-ee53649fe5aa$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$cfaeeb6b-3fc4-4a81-82dd-d5506ec5a318$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>British musicians John Lennon and Paul McCartney shared writing credit for numerous Beatles songs.</li>
<li>Many Lennon-McCartney songs were actually written by either Lennon or McCartney, not by both.</li>
<li>The exact authorship of specific parts of many Beatles songs, such as the verse for &ldquo;In My Life,&rdquo; is disputed.</li>
<li>Mark Glickman, Jason Brown, and Ryan Song used statistical methods to analyze the musical content of Beatles songs.</li>
<li>They concluded that there is 18.9% probability that McCartney wrote the verse for &ldquo;In My Life,&rdquo; stating that the verse is &ldquo;consistent with Lennon&rsquo;s songwriting style.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to make a generalization about the kind of study conducted by Glickman, Brown, and Song. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"446c10cd-38d2-4637-9bf8-9b3a7ffd2a97","content":"<p>Based on statistical analysis, Glickman, Brown, and Song claim that John Lennon wrote the verse of &ldquo;In My Life.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"477fd5b4-9ad2-4843-8c45-1e512aff7799","content":"<p>There is only an 18.9% probability that Paul McCartney wrote the verse for &ldquo;In My Life&rdquo;; John Lennon is the more likely author.</p>"},{"id":"c0e616e9-86fa-4905-a517-47efc5f71c4b","content":"<p>It is likely that John Lennon, not Paul McCartney, wrote the verse for &ldquo;In My Life.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"c3e0f6eb-030c-42bd-be16-07d3331469a0","content":"<p>Researchers have used statistical methods to address questions of authorship within the field of music.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c3e0f6eb-030c-42bd-be16-07d3331469a0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence uses information from the notes to make a generalization about the kind of study Glickman, Brown, and Song conducted. Specifically, the sentence indicates that the study was of a kind that used statistical methods to address questions of authorship within the field of music. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the sentence summarizes the methodology and findings of a particular analysis of a single song; it doesn&rsquo;t make a generalization about the kind of study conducted. Choice B is incorrect because the sentence mentions the data and conclusion of a particular analysis of a single song; it doesn&rsquo;t make a generalization about the kind of study conducted. Choice C is incorrect because the sentence focuses on a specific conclusion from a particular analysis of a single song; it doesn&rsquo;t make a generalization about the kind of study conducted. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-705-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$4586b5a5-5726-44cc-aea0-5d38b7d12b12$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2c61e0b9","external_id":"91d983a0-c121-4eb2-a3cf-ee53649fe5aa","vaultid":"cfaeeb6b-3fc4-4a81-82dd-d5506ec5a318","uId":"4aa9e482-d062-4dee-a48b-d734e0aa2953","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>British musicians John Lennon and Paul McCartney shared writing credit for numerous Beatles songs.</li>\n<li>Many Lennon-McCartney songs were actually written by either Lennon or McCartney, not by both.</li>\n<li>The exact authorship of specific parts of many Beatles songs, such as the verse for &ldquo;In My Life,&rdquo; is disputed.</li>\n<li>Mark Glickman, Jason Brown, and Ryan Song used statistical methods to analyze the musical content of Beatles songs.</li>\n<li>They concluded that there is 18.9% probability that McCartney wrote the verse for &ldquo;In My Life,&rdquo; stating that the verse is &ldquo;consistent with Lennon&rsquo;s songwriting style.&rdquo;</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to make a generalization about the kind of study conducted by Glickman, Brown, and Song. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"446c10cd-38d2-4637-9bf8-9b3a7ffd2a97","content":"<p>Based on statistical analysis, Glickman, Brown, and Song claim that John Lennon wrote the verse of &ldquo;In My Life.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"477fd5b4-9ad2-4843-8c45-1e512aff7799","content":"<p>There is only an 18.9% probability that Paul McCartney wrote the verse for &ldquo;In My Life&rdquo;; John Lennon is the more likely author.</p>"},{"id":"c0e616e9-86fa-4905-a517-47efc5f71c4b","content":"<p>It is likely that John Lennon, not Paul McCartney, wrote the verse for &ldquo;In My Life.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"c3e0f6eb-030c-42bd-be16-07d3331469a0","content":"<p>Researchers have used statistical methods to address questions of authorship within the field of music.</p>"}],"keys":["c3e0f6eb-030c-42bd-be16-07d3331469a0"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence uses information from the notes to make a generalization about the kind of study Glickman, Brown, and Song conducted. Specifically, the sentence indicates that the study was of a kind that used statistical methods to address questions of authorship within the field of music. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because the sentence summarizes the methodology and findings of a particular analysis of a single song; it doesn&rsquo;t make a generalization about the kind of study conducted. Choice B is incorrect because the sentence mentions the data and conclusion of a particular analysis of a single song; it doesn&rsquo;t make a generalization about the kind of study conducted. Choice C is incorrect because the sentence focuses on a specific conclusion from a particular analysis of a single song; it doesn&rsquo;t make a generalization about the kind of study conducted. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-705-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"4586b5a5-5726-44cc-aea0-5d38b7d12b12","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$2c7dced2$SATQ$, $SATQ$3f43e761-1851-44dc-8616-1d1e3fb25a96$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sister Rosetta Tharpe (1915&ndash;1973) was a gospel musician.</li>
<li>She was known for her passionate vocals and electric guitar performances.</li>
<li>In 2018, Tharpe was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame for her major impact on the genre.</li>
<li>According to songwriter Roxie Moore, &ldquo;[Tharpe] would sing until you cried and then she would sing until you danced for joy.&rdquo;</li>
<li>According to guitarist Celisse Henderson, &ldquo;Tharpe is the unquestioned founding mother of rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to use a quotation to support a claim about Tharpe&rsquo;s contribution to rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4a31638f-c283-4356-a8a4-8be81a99fcbc","content":"<p>Gospel musician Sister Rosetta Tharpe had a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, and she was known for her passionate electric guitar performances.</p>"},{"id":"bf165de0-ee68-4e2e-8a33-84f1924f6ffc","content":"<p>Celisse Henderson believes that Sister Rosetta Tharpe had a major impact on the development of rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.</p>"},{"id":"069ecee9-d91e-4e7c-846d-34a6c8404ce5","content":"<p>Sister Rosetta Tharpe had such a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll that Celisse Henderson called her &ldquo;the unquestioned founding mother&rdquo; of the genre.</p>"},{"id":"7d8fa30f-6ca0-4fa8-abe5-9ce783cf74f5","content":"<p>A gospel musician, Sister Rosetta Tharpe had the ability to &ldquo;sing until you cried&rdquo; and also &ldquo;until you danced for joy,&rdquo; according to Roxie Moore.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["069ecee9-d91e-4e7c-846d-34a6c8404ce5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence uses the quotation from Henderson to support a claim about Tharpe&rsquo;s contribution to rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, noting that the impact Tharpe had on the genre led Henderson to call her "the unquestioned founding mother of rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence makes the claim that Tharpe had a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, it doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to support this claim. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence presents Henderson&rsquo;s opinion that Tharpe had a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, but it doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to support this claim. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence includes a quotation about audience reactions to Tharpe&rsquo;s music, the sentence describes Tharpe as a gospel musician; it doesn&rsquo;t support a claim about her contribution to rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2c7dced2","external_id":"3f43e761-1851-44dc-8616-1d1e3fb25a96","vaultid":null,"uId":"deea55ac-d587-4198-9ea9-350ded68cd9b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sister Rosetta Tharpe (1915&ndash;1973) was a gospel musician.</li>\n<li>She was known for her passionate vocals and electric guitar performances.</li>\n<li>In 2018, Tharpe was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame for her major impact on the genre.</li>\n<li>According to songwriter Roxie Moore, &ldquo;[Tharpe] would sing until you cried and then she would sing until you danced for joy.&rdquo;</li>\n<li>According to guitarist Celisse Henderson, &ldquo;Tharpe is the unquestioned founding mother of rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.&rdquo;</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to use a quotation to support a claim about Tharpe&rsquo;s contribution to rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4a31638f-c283-4356-a8a4-8be81a99fcbc","content":"<p>Gospel musician Sister Rosetta Tharpe had a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, and she was known for her passionate electric guitar performances.</p>"},{"id":"bf165de0-ee68-4e2e-8a33-84f1924f6ffc","content":"<p>Celisse Henderson believes that Sister Rosetta Tharpe had a major impact on the development of rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.</p>"},{"id":"069ecee9-d91e-4e7c-846d-34a6c8404ce5","content":"<p>Sister Rosetta Tharpe had such a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll that Celisse Henderson called her &ldquo;the unquestioned founding mother&rdquo; of the genre.</p>"},{"id":"7d8fa30f-6ca0-4fa8-abe5-9ce783cf74f5","content":"<p>A gospel musician, Sister Rosetta Tharpe had the ability to &ldquo;sing until you cried&rdquo; and also &ldquo;until you danced for joy,&rdquo; according to Roxie Moore.</p>"}],"keys":["069ecee9-d91e-4e7c-846d-34a6c8404ce5"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence uses the quotation from Henderson to support a claim about Tharpe&rsquo;s contribution to rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, noting that the impact Tharpe had on the genre led Henderson to call her \"the unquestioned founding mother of rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence makes the claim that Tharpe had a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, it doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to support this claim. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence presents Henderson&rsquo;s opinion that Tharpe had a major impact on rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll, but it doesn&rsquo;t use a quotation to support this claim. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence includes a quotation about audience reactions to Tharpe&rsquo;s music, the sentence describes Tharpe as a gospel musician; it doesn&rsquo;t support a claim about her contribution to rock &rsquo;n&rsquo; roll.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$2df7b582$SATQ$, $SATQ$7bd46d33-dddf-4a50-a090-87e6e1f750ab$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$ab32ddf8-3173-49f9-a0b0-849cb9049d1d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Plato believed material objects to be crude representations of unseen ideal forms. In his view, such abstract, nonmaterial forms are the ultimate source of knowledge. Aristotle disagreed, positing that knowledge is best obtained through direct engagement with the material world; <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> sensory experience of the material is the ultimate source of knowledge.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9c9abcc3-6729-4284-8649-93bc89969530","content":"<p>regardless,</p>"},{"id":"aac9596c-8f66-4450-8454-0aa09b00cd6b","content":"<p>admittedly,</p>"},{"id":"ee68b669-8a3f-4ba5-8587-71347a5cc970","content":"<p>in other words,</p>"},{"id":"2ff9da0a-6a81-42e9-94e8-53cb11a5a5be","content":"<p>meanwhile,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ee68b669-8a3f-4ba5-8587-71347a5cc970"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;In other words&rdquo; logically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows&mdash;that sensory experience is the source of knowledge&mdash;is a restatement of Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;regardless&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows is true in spite of Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. Instead, this claim is a restatement of his theory. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;admittedly&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows is an exception to Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. Instead, this claim is a restatement of his theory. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows is separate from (while occurring simultaneously with) Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. Instead, this claim is a restatement of his theory. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-H005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$1021858a-a15b-4f85-9955-8490c53fe15c$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"2df7b582","external_id":"7bd46d33-dddf-4a50-a090-87e6e1f750ab","vaultid":"ab32ddf8-3173-49f9-a0b0-849cb9049d1d","uId":"60f3db51-1f64-43f2-a11b-04ec155a82e5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Plato believed material objects to be crude representations of unseen ideal forms. In his view, such abstract, nonmaterial forms are the ultimate source of knowledge. Aristotle disagreed, positing that knowledge is best obtained through direct engagement with the material world; <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> sensory experience of the material is the ultimate source of knowledge.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9c9abcc3-6729-4284-8649-93bc89969530","content":"<p>regardless,</p>"},{"id":"aac9596c-8f66-4450-8454-0aa09b00cd6b","content":"<p>admittedly,</p>"},{"id":"ee68b669-8a3f-4ba5-8587-71347a5cc970","content":"<p>in other words,</p>"},{"id":"2ff9da0a-6a81-42e9-94e8-53cb11a5a5be","content":"<p>meanwhile,</p>"}],"keys":["ee68b669-8a3f-4ba5-8587-71347a5cc970"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;In other words&rdquo; logically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows&mdash;that sensory experience is the source of knowledge&mdash;is a restatement of Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;regardless&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows is true in spite of Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. Instead, this claim is a restatement of his theory. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;admittedly&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows is an exception to Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. Instead, this claim is a restatement of his theory. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim about sensory experience that follows is separate from (while occurring simultaneously with) Aristotle&rsquo;s theory from earlier in the sentence. Instead, this claim is a restatement of his theory. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-H005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"1021858a-a15b-4f85-9955-8490c53fe15c","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$30438650$SATQ$, $SATQ$659659ad-43c6-4be1-9e53-5f5814d3c4e9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$53002d7f-61f8-432b-96c8-2a4d1ebd83f5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Jhumpa Lahiri&rsquo;s story collection <em>Interpreter of Maladies</em> features multiple stories about romantic relationships. In &ldquo;This Blessed House,&rdquo; newlyweds argue over whether to replace items left by the previous owners of their new home. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> in &ldquo;A Temporary Matter,&rdquo; a husband and wife attempt to rekindle their relationship during a four-night blackout.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"55341d44-515e-4e59-893c-1dab2d0fa845","content":"<p>Granted,</p>"},{"id":"e49faa50-393b-4cf5-8cd7-e3fcde3afff3","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"67f4d2c2-6ce3-41c3-8fc2-8f9339f9880d","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"6b514e16-cc81-42c5-9b12-8905e13fc228","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["67f4d2c2-6ce3-41c3-8fc2-8f9339f9880d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "Likewise" is a transition that indicates the addition of a new but related idea. In this sentence, the author is providing another similar example to that discussed in the previous sentence. Therefore, "likewise" works best in this context.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that means "admittedly." But the text isn&rsquo;t admitting or conceding anything here. Instead, these two examples work together to support the claim made in the first sentence. Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses an exemplification transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The second story is not an example of the story in the previous sentence&mdash;it&rsquo;s another, similar story. And while both stories exemplify the first sentence in the text, the transition we&rsquo;re looking for isn&rsquo;t actually connected to that sentence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a cause-and-effect transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The first story didn&rsquo;t result in the events of the second story.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-M005$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$d87a4b9c-0255-4ee7-a144-b4f91b379e85$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"30438650","external_id":"659659ad-43c6-4be1-9e53-5f5814d3c4e9","vaultid":"53002d7f-61f8-432b-96c8-2a4d1ebd83f5","uId":"2eb8f8d5-8259-4ce4-bcfc-9a8d327e8d2f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Jhumpa Lahiri&rsquo;s story collection <em>Interpreter of Maladies</em> features multiple stories about romantic relationships. In &ldquo;This Blessed House,&rdquo; newlyweds argue over whether to replace items left by the previous owners of their new home. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> in &ldquo;A Temporary Matter,&rdquo; a husband and wife attempt to rekindle their relationship during a four-night blackout.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"55341d44-515e-4e59-893c-1dab2d0fa845","content":"<p>Granted,</p>"},{"id":"e49faa50-393b-4cf5-8cd7-e3fcde3afff3","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"67f4d2c2-6ce3-41c3-8fc2-8f9339f9880d","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"6b514e16-cc81-42c5-9b12-8905e13fc228","content":"<p>Hence,</p>"}],"keys":["67f4d2c2-6ce3-41c3-8fc2-8f9339f9880d"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"Likewise\" is a transition that indicates the addition of a new but related idea. In this sentence, the author is providing another similar example to that discussed in the previous sentence. Therefore, \"likewise\" works best in this context.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that means \"admittedly.\" But the text isn&rsquo;t admitting or conceding anything here. Instead, these two examples work together to support the claim made in the first sentence. Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses an exemplification transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The second story is not an example of the story in the previous sentence&mdash;it&rsquo;s another, similar story. And while both stories exemplify the first sentence in the text, the transition we&rsquo;re looking for isn&rsquo;t actually connected to that sentence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a cause-and-effect transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The first story didn&rsquo;t result in the events of the second story.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-M005","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"d87a4b9c-0255-4ee7-a144-b4f91b379e85","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$3067723b$SATQ$, $SATQ$b93814bc-b492-4f7a-8521-3d18602dbe18$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$24fe0889-4c1c-4f5d-af96-2f1866ba1deb$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Seikan Tunnel is a rail tunnel in Japan.</li>
<li>It connects the island of Honshu to the island of Hokkaido.</li>
<li>It is roughly 33 miles long.</li>
<li>The Channel Tunnel is a rail tunnel in Europe.</li>
<li>It connects Folkestone, England, to Coquelles, France.</li>
<li>It is about 31 miles long.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to compare the lengths of the two rail tunnels. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b4ba2088-b8c4-4166-a72b-2c485af42715","content":"<p>Some of the world&rsquo;s rail tunnels,&nbsp;including one tunnel that extends from Folkestone, England, to Coquelles, France, are longer than 30 miles.</p>"},{"id":"20bb9e00-ec31-4d92-b70b-f42187a3edfb","content":"<p>The Seikan Tunnel is roughly 33 miles long, while the slightly shorter Channel Tunnel is about 31 miles long.</p>"},{"id":"cb82b10d-5fb6-4c71-ad2f-f67ef0f0d81f","content":"<p>The Seikan Tunnel, which is roughly 33 miles long, connects the Japanese islands of Honshu and Hokkaido.</p>"},{"id":"0134f8f0-5ab5-47a0-856c-be762535758d","content":"<p>Both the Seikan Tunnel, which is located in Japan, and the Channel Tunnel, which is located in Europe, are examples of rail tunnels.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["20bb9e00-ec31-4d92-b70b-f42187a3edfb"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence compares the lengths of the two rail tunnels, noting that the Channel Tunnel (about 31 miles long) is slightly shorter than the Seikan Tunnel (roughly 33 miles long).&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence makes a generalization about the length of some rail tunnels; it doesn&rsquo;t compare the lengths of the two rail tunnels. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes a single rail tunnel; it doesn&rsquo;t compare the lengths of the two rail tunnels. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence mentions the two rail tunnels, it doesn&rsquo;t compare their lengths. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-767-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$2bbb2992-b3a3-4bed-8e5e-a9e185a872fe$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"3067723b","external_id":"b93814bc-b492-4f7a-8521-3d18602dbe18","vaultid":"24fe0889-4c1c-4f5d-af96-2f18